# Compression and prediction

**Compression and prediction** are linked by an exact result: a model that
predicts a sequence well, measured by logarithmic loss, yields a short code
for that sequence, and the reverse. The link is a theorem of coding. The
larger claim often attached to it, that predictability, compressibility and
intelligence are one and the same, is a conjecture, and this card keeps the
two apart.

## Description

### The conjecture as it was raised

The discussion reached this topic from quines. A program that prints itself,
or translates itself into another language, is a feat of construction, and
with language models one no longer needs that trick: a model continues or
translates a text without any self-reproducing design. That led to the
conjecture that predictability is equivalent to compressibility, with
compression in turn conjectured to be equivalent to intelligence.

A related intuition had appeared earlier, among the proposed criteria for a
[[strange-loop]]: such a system would be "irreducible in some sense", unable
to be compressed to rudimentary computations, while still capturing the
patterns it describes.

### What is exact

For a probabilistic model $P$ of a sequence, the length in bits of the code
it induces is

$$L(x_{1:n})\approx-\sum_{t=1}^n\log_2 P(x_t\mid x_{<t}).$$

Each symbol costs the negative logarithm of the probability the model gave
it. A model that assigns high probability to what actually comes next spends
few bits. Under suitable coding assumptions, good [[prediction]] under
logarithmic loss and short description are the same quantity read two ways.

### What is not established

The reply set three limits on the conjecture.

- The formula does not prove a universal equivalence of predictability and
  compressibility. It relates one loss to one kind of code.
- It does not show that compression is identical to intelligence. That
  remains a conjecture.
- "Irreducible" is ambiguous. Algorithmic incompressibility, which is about
  the shortest description of an object, is a different notion from
  [[computational-irreducibility]], which is about shortcuts to a
  computation, and both differ from [[undecidability]]. Gödel's theorem
  concerns formal incompleteness and says nothing general about compression.

### The cycle with a second loop

What survives is a picture of how the operations fit together.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[Observations] --> B[Discover regularities]
 B --> C[Compress / build model]
 C --> D[Predict outcomes]
 D --> E[Choose action and evaluate]
 E --> A
 E --> F[Build a model of the modeling process]
 F --> B
```

The outer loop is the [[reasoning-cycle]] in other words. Discovering
regularities is [[induction]], and a discovered regularity is a shorter
description, which is a model, which predicts.

The inner loop is the part that matters for this vault. The system models
its own modelling and uses that to change how it finds regularities. The
outer loop alone is a [[feedback-loop]]. The inner one passes through a
representation of the process itself, which is the mark of a
[[reflective-agent]] and the place a [[level-crossing]] could occur.

## Relations

- Its exact half is a statement about [[prediction]] {relation: uses} under logarithmic loss.
- Its model-building step is [[induction]] {relation: uses}: to discover a regularity is to compress.
- Its outer loop is the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: analogous-to} in the vocabulary of coding.
- Its inner loop is a small [[reflective-agent]] {relation: example-of}.
- It is not [[computational-irreducibility]] {relation: distinguished-from}: incompressible data and unshortenable computation are different limits.
- It is not [[undecidability]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which concerns what no procedure settles.
- It was reached from the [[quine]] {relation: contrasts}: reproduction by construction, against continuation by a model.
- Its outer loop alone is a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: is-a}.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[prediction]].** The operation from a history and a model to expected
  observations. Here it is the measure by which a compressing model is
  scored.
- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis, always
  paired with deduction. It is the step this card calls discovering
  regularities.
- **[[computational-irreducibility]].** The absence of a substantial
  shortcut to the outcome of a computation. It is one of three notions hidden
  in the word "irreducible", and not the one compression measures.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** A system that revises its own rules from a model
  of itself. The second loop of the diagram is its simplest form.

